 "title": "Laws of exponents",
 "audience": "learner",
 "keyIdea": "When you multiply powers of the same base you add the powers, when you divide you subtract them, and a power of a power multiplies them.",
 "explain": {
  "kid": [
   "x³ means x × x × x. The small number counts how many x's are multiplied.",
   "x³ × x⁵ puts 3 x's next to 5 more. That is 8 x's, so it is x⁸. You add the powers."
  ],
  "adult": [
   "Multiply: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ. Divide: aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, because n factors cancel. Power of a power: (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ.",
   "The laws need the same base. 2³ × 3² cannot be combined this way. A common slip is x³ × x⁵ = x¹⁵. Counting the factors shows it is x⁸."
  ]
 },
